      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of distinct numbers in the list\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\dots, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify each term in the list. For a given \\( n \\), we have\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nWe need to analyze how these terms change as \\( n \\) varies from 1 to 1000.\n\nFor any \\( n \\), let \\( k = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\). This means\n\\[\nn^2 \\leq 1000k < (n+1)^2.\n\\]\nRewriting this inequality, we get\n\\[\nn^2 \\leq 1000k \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (n+1)^2 > 1000k.\n\\]\nTaking the square root of all parts of the inequality gives\n\\[\nn \\leq \\sqrt{1000k} < n+1.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to\n\\[\nn - \\sqrt{1000k} \\leq 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sqrt{1000k+1} > n.\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\nn - \\sqrt{1000k} \\leq 0 \\implies n \\leq \\sqrt{1000k}, \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sqrt{1000k+1} > n.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{1000k} < n \\leq \\sqrt{1000k+1}.\n\\]\nThis shows that \\( k \\) increments by 1 every time \\( n \\) adds (approximately) 1000 to \\( n \\).\n\nNext, we calculate \\( \\sqrt{1000k} \\) and \\( \\sqrt{1000k+1} \\) for \\( k = 1, 2, \\ldots, 1000 \\):\n- For \\( k = 1 \\),\n  \\[\n  \\sqrt{1000 \\cdot 1} = \\sqrt{1000} \\approx 31.62.\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 2 \\),\n  \\[\n  \\sqrt{1000 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{2000} \\approx 44.72.\n  \\]\n\n- And so on until\n  \\[\n  \\sqrt{1000 \\cdot 1000} = 1000.\n  \\]\n\nHence, \\( k \\) ranges from 1 to 1000, inclusive. This implies that each \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\) for \\( n \\) from 1 to 1000 results in a different value of \\( k \\) because \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\) for consecutive \\( n \\) values increases by 1 when \\( n \\) grows to the next even number or odd number.\n\nSo, there are exactly 1000 distinct values in the list.\n\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{1000}\\).",
