      "text": "Since each of the numbers is found by evaluating a quadratic function\n\\[f(x) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\]\nfor $x$ from 1 to 1000 being both positive and an integer, thus giving 999 distinct numbers including the number for $f(x)$. Therefore, the number of distinct numbers in the given list is $999.$\n\nTo find the number of distinct numbers in the given list, we need to evaluate the function $f(x) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor$ for $x$ from 1 to 1000 and count the number of distinct values it can take.\n\nFor $x = 1$, we have $f(1) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0$.\nFor $x = 2$, we have $f(2) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{4}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0$.\nFor $x = 3$, we have $f(3) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{9}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0$.\nContinuing this pattern, we can see that for any integer $x$ where $1 \\le x \\le 1000$, $f(x) = 0$.\n\nSo, only one distinct number, namely 0, exists in the given list.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct numbers in the list is $\\boxed{1}$.",
